# When a user types www.youtube.com
# into a browser, several steps occur before the video appears on the screen.

# Step 1: The Client (Browser)
# The browser (Chrome, Edge, Firefox, etc.) acts as the client.
# When the user enters:
# www.youtube.com
# The browser:
# Checks its cache to see if it already knows the IP address.
# If not, it sends a request to the DNS server to find the IP address of the domain.

# Step 2: DNS Resolution
# The DNS (Domain Name System) works like the phonebook of the internet.
# Example:
# www.youtube.com → 142.250.xxx.xxx
# Process:
# The browser asks the DNS resolver (usually provided by the ISP).
# The resolver queries DNS servers until it finds the correct IP address.
# The DNS returns the IP address to the browser.

# Step 3: Using the IP Address
# Once the browser receives the IP address, it now knows which server to contact.
# The browser establishes a connection using:
# TCP/IP
# Port 443 (HTTPS)

# Step 4: HTTP / HTTPS Protocol
# The browser sends a request to the YouTube server using HTTP or HTTPS.
# Example request:
# GET / HTTP/1.1
# Host: www.youtube.com
# With HTTPS, communication is encrypted using SSL/TLS, which protects the data being transmitted.

# Step 5: Server Response
# The YouTube server receives the request and:
# Processes the request.
#Retrieves information from databases.
# Prepares the webpage content.
# The server sends back:
# 1-HTML
# 2-CSS
# 3-JavaScript
# 4-Video data
# 5-Media files

# Step 6: Rendering in the Browser
# The browser receives the files and:
# Interprets HTML (structure)
# Applies CSS (design)
# Executes JavaScript (interactivity)
# Loads the video stream
# Finally, the video appears on the screen.

# Communication Diagram
# User
# │
# ▼
# Browser (Client)
# │
# │ DNS Request
#  ▼
# DNS Server
# │
# │ Returns IP
# ▼
# YouTube Server
# │
# │ HTTP/HTTPS Response
# ▼
# Browser renders page
# │
# ▼
# Video appears
